 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Sydney rents are still rising, but much more slowly than at the peak. They remain far higher than a few years ago.**\n\n**Current pace (ABS CPI rents, August 2026)**\n- Sydney rents are up **3.5%** on a year earlier. The figure has been steady at 3.3–3.5% since about mid-2025.\n- That is slightly slower than Sydney's overall inflation. The all-groups index rose about 3.9% over the same 12 months, going from 99.69 to 103.61 (my calculation from the index).\n- Sydney is a little above the national rent figure (3.6%) and Melbourne (2.5%). It is below Brisbane (4.7%) and Perth (5.3%).\n\n**The peak**\n- Annual rent inflation in Sydney peaked at about **8.9% in Q1 2024**. It was 8.8% in the monthly series for March 2024.\n- It was above 8% from about Q3 2023 to mid-2024.\n- It then fell to about 3.2% by August–September 2025 and has stayed near 3.5% since.\n\n**The cumulative damage**\n- Sydney's rent index was flat to falling in 2020–21. It bottomed at 114.0 in Q4 2021 and reached 139.5 by Q3 2025, a rise of about **22%** in under four years.\n- Another 3.3% has been added since September 2025 (the monthly index went from 100.0 to 103.29). Chaining the two series, which use different bases, gives roughly **26% above the late-2021 low**.\n- Over a similar period, Sydney's overall CPI rose about 18% to Q3 2025, so rents have outpaced general prices.\n\n**Bottom line:** The worst of the acceleration is over. Renters are no longer seeing 8–9% annual jumps, and rents are now rising at about the same pace as everything else. But the earlier increases have not reversed, so rents stay high relative to incomes.\n\nThese figures are CPI rents, which cover all existing tenancies. Asking rents on new listings can be higher, so people moving house may see bigger jumps than the CPI suggests. The monthly CPI data starts in 2023, and I used the quarterly series for earlier periods.",
